=\"sig\">108 record highs</strong>, far more than the 64 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (53). La Crosse's all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on July 14, 1936.","t_chg":"La Crosse has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 151 complete years: La Crosse's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.10\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.17), about +1.5\u00b0F since 1873. La Crosse's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.23\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.29), about +3.5\u00b0F since 1873.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 3 of 12 months. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"La Crosse has gotten wetter","p_rain":"La Crosse's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.25 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.04 to +0.47), about +3.8 inches since 1873. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 35.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 2016 (44.76 in); the driest, 1910 (16.77 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}